final_answer:
1. Set resizable properties using Java API:

   // Create a TextArea
   TextArea textArea = new TextArea();

   // Set resizable properties
   textArea.setResizable(true);
   textArea.setMinWidth(200);
   textArea.setMaxWidth(400);
   textArea.setMinHeight(100);
   textArea.setMaxHeight(300);

2. Apply CSS styling to define width, height, and maximum size:

   .text-area {
       -fx-width: 200px;
       -fx-height: 100px;
       -fx-max-width: 400px;
       -fx-max-height: 300px;
   }

3. Define scrollable features in FXML:

   <ScrollPane>
       <TextArea fx:controller="com.example.TextAreaController"
                 fx:id="textArea"
                 styleClass="text-area"
                 prefWidth="200"
                 prefHeight="100"
                 maxWidth="400"
                 maxHeight="300"
                 wrapText="true"
                 scrollBarPolicy="alwaysOn">
       </TextArea>
   </ScrollPane>

4. Considerations for Scene Builder and IDE usage:

   - In Scene Builder, set the preferred size and maximum size for the TextArea.
   - Ensure the TextArea is placed inside a ScrollPane for scrolling functionality.
   - Link the FXML file to the corresponding Java class in your IDE.
   - Use the CSS file to apply styling to the TextArea.

5. Step-by-step implementation guide:

   a. Create a new JavaFX project in your IDE.
   b. Add a ScrollPane to your FXML file and place a TextArea inside it.
   c. Apply CSS styling to the TextArea in a CSS file.
   d. Use the Java API to set resizable properties and size constraints.
   e. Test the TextArea in the IDE and Scene Builder to ensure it is scrollable and resizable within the defined limits.

